# rlfixer.msg - Frankie "Two Chairs" Pagano, go-between of the New Reno families
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

{100}{}{(A man in a good suit sits at a card table with his back to the wall. The chair next to him is empty, and nobody sits on it.) Frankie Pagano. They call me Two Chairs, because I sit at every family's table and I get up from all of them. You want the Garter. You need two things: paper, and a blessing.}
{101}{}{About the deed.}
{102}{}{About Bishop's letter.}
{103}{}{About the families.}
{104}{}{See you around, Frankie.}
{105}{}{Chair?}
{106}{}{You've got the paper. Now you need somebody's blessing. Or nobody's.}
{107}{}{You've got a blessing. Now get the paper, and the house is yours.}
{109}{}{(Somebody with a lantern saw you. By noon, Virgin Street knows.)}

# The deed
{110}{}{The widow Varga has it, upstairs in the old bridal suite. She'll sell, if you ask the right way. If you don't want to ask, there's always a man who can draw a deed.}
{111}{}{The widow's dead, and she knew where the only copy was. Well. Her husband has the original. He took it with him to Golgotha, third row from the wall. If you don't want to dig, there's always a man who can draw a deed.}
{112}{}{[Science] Deeds get drawn all the time. What do I need?}
{113}{}{[Dig up Varga's grave in Golgotha tonight.]}
{114}{}{Let's talk about something else.}
{115}{}{Paper with the city watermark. The Golden Globes print their contracts on it, and a boy there sells me a stack whenever he needs a fix. A hundred. The signature is your problem.}
{116}{}{Here's a hundred. Get me the paper.}
{117}{}{(Two nights later the deed lies on the table, the Varga signature in faded ink, the watermark clear against the lamp. It's good work. Good work gets found out, sometimes.)}
{118}{}{Now the blessing.}
{119}{}{(Golgotha after midnight. The ground above Varga is soft. The deed is in his breast pocket, folded twice. You fill the hole again before dawn.)}

# The families
{120}{}{Four families, four prices. Big Jesus Mordino sells Jet out of the Desperado. Orville Wright hates Jet more than he loves money. Salvatore does business with men in black armor and never says which men. And Bishop owns the Shark Club and half the NCR council. Pick one. The one you pick will like you. The others will remember.}
{121}{}{Mordino.}
{122}{}{Wright.}
{123}{}{Salvatore.}
{124}{}{Bishop.}
{125}{}{I'm already family. Tell them.}
{126}{}{What if I don't want any of them?}
{139}{}{Let me think about the families.}

{130}{}{Big Jesus has a shipment that needs to reach the Stables, and the only road runs past the Wright boys. Get it there, and you have Mordino's blessing. The Wrights will know who carried it.}
{131}{}{[Sneak] I'll take it through the back alleys at night.}
{132}{}{[Combat] I'll take it straight past the Wright boys.}

{140}{}{Orville Wright's boy died of Jet. Orville thinks Mordino Jet is going through the Cat's Paw, right under Miss Kitty's nose. Bring him proof and you have his blessing. Kitty will find out who went looking. She always does.}
{141}{}{[Perception] I'll sit at the Cat's Paw bar and watch.}
{142}{}{[Steal] I'll lift the proof out of somebody's pocket.}

{150}{}{Salvatore has an exchange in the desert tonight. He never says with whom. He needs one more gun who doesn't ask questions. Come back without questions, and you have his blessing.}
{151}{}{[Combat] I'll stand guard.}
{152}{}{I've guarded Salvatore's exchange before. Ask him.}

{160}{}{Bishop needs a letter taken to Westin, on his ranch in the NCR. Sealed. You don't open it, you don't read it, you don't lose it. Bring me word that it arrived, and you have Bishop's blessing.}
{161}{}{Give me the letter.}
{162}{}{[Lockpick] (Take the letter. Tonight you lift the seal with a hot knife.)}
{163}{}{(He puts an envelope with a wax seal on the table.) Westin's ranch, NCR. And Bishop would know if the wax had been warm.}
{164}{}{(Later, alone, you lift the seal with a hot knife. It isn't a letter. It's a list: NCR councilmen, and what each of them costs per month. Next to Westin's name there are two prices. One for a yes, and one for a no. You seal it again. Nobody could tell. Somebody always can.)}
{167}{}{Westin got it. Bishop is pleased, and a pleased Bishop pays in favors. Doesn't matter now, you already belong to somebody else. But people in the NCR will remember your face.}
{168}{}{Westin's ranch, NCR. Until that letter is there, we have nothing to talk about.}

# Nobody's blessing: the opening night
{180}{}{Nobody's blessing. (He shakes his head slowly.) Then you open without anybody's colors on the door, and on the first night everybody will want to see if you survive it. Men without colors will come. Nobody will know who paid them. Everybody will know.}
{181}{}{Nobody opens a house without paper. First the deed. Then we can talk about how you want to die.}
{182}{}{Let them come.}
{183}{}{(Pagano stands up and takes his hat.) Then I'd better not be sitting here tonight. (He looks around the empty lobby.) Nice place. Would be a shame.}
{184}{}{...}

# The blessing (190 + family)
{191}{}{(The shipment reached the Stables. Nobody saw you, or everybody did; in New Reno it's the same thing.) Big Jesus sends his regards and a bottle. The Wrights are sending nothing, and that's worse.}
{192}{}{(Two nights later Orville Wright holds the proof in his hands for a long time.) He shook my hand and didn't let go for a while. You have his blessing. Kitty knows your name now.}
{193}{}{(A night in the desert. Men in black armor, a crate, no words. Nobody asks you anything on the way back.) Salvatore said nothing. That's his blessing. The men in black armor don't exist, and you were never in the desert.}
{194}{}{Bishop says thank you. He never says thank you. You have his blessing.}
{196}{}{Now the paper. Without the deed, a blessing is just a family that knows your name.}
{197}{}{Deed and blessing. The Silver Garter is yours. I'm sending you Roz Mercer to run it: ten years dealing cards at the Shark Club, and she knows who cheats before they do. And Leopold Asch for the books. He's kept them for three families and outlived two. (He stands up.) Don't thank me. You'll get my bill.}
{198}{}{Goodbye, Frankie.}

# Look
{300}{}{A man in a good suit next to an empty chair.}
{301}{}{Frankie Pagano, called Two Chairs. He sits at every family's table, and he has never stood up poorer.}
